From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Emmanuel Velez Garcia <velezgarciaemmanuel25@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 09:43:04 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] Named knockback profiles

The fork has three knockback models and around forty tunable values, and
no way to reach a sensible starting point for a given style of server
short of typing all of them.

/knockback profile lists what is available; /knockback profile <name>
[world|default] applies one:

  DEFAULT   vanilla 1.8, untouched - the way back to a clean slate
  HCF       grounded, with the 1.7 chase reduction on so a chase can
            actually be closed
  PRACTICE  CONSTANT model, so every hit sends the victim the same
            distance and combos are repeatable
  POT       more vertical and air time, rods and pearls carry the fight
  OP        short knockback with combo hold, for heavily geared fights

A profile is applied once and written to knockback.yml like any
hand-typed value - a starting point, not a mode the server keeps
enforcing. That matters because the in-game editor writes to the same
file, and a profile that re-asserted itself would silently undo whatever
was tuned afterwards.

Applying one builds a fresh KnockbackConfig rather than editing in
place, so it is a genuine clean slate for that world, and the command
says so when it confirms. "default" targets the section every world
without its own entry falls back to.

These numbers are opinionated, not measured: recognisable starting
points built from knobs this fork already has, not reproductions of any
particular network's feel. The class javadoc says so rather than
implying otherwise.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

